** Protein (sp|O14594|NCAN_HUMAN) has only single transition : Consider excluding this protein from the dataset.

  Summary of Samples :
                           126/127 126/128 131/129 131/130
# of MS runs                     1       1       1       1
# of Biological Replicates       1       1       1       1
# of Technical Replicates        0       0       0       0

 Summary of Missingness :

  # transitions are completely missing in one condition: 3

    -> (TMT6plex)LFDAHVVYEAENETPLLYIDR_3_1_NA, (TMT6plex)TGSAGGTGHVVEFC(Carbamidomethyl)GEAIR_4_1_NA, (TMT6plex)SHRMRRHHHHHQ(Deamidated)HHHQHHHHK(TMT6plex)SRK(TMT6plex)ERRK(TMT6plex)_4_1_NA
  # run with 75% missing observations: 0

> dataProcessPlots(data=part.proc, type="ConditionPlot", address=paste0(base.dir, "/"))
Error in data.frame(Label = unique(sub.l$GROUP_ORIGINAL), mean = as.vector(sub.mean),  :
  arguments imply differing number of rows: 3, 4
> part.quant <- quantification(part.proc)
Error in `contrasts<-`(`*tmp*`, value = contr.funs[1 + isOF[nn]]) :
  contrasts can be applied only to factors with 2 or more levels

For "dataProcessPlots", you might consider wrapping the code for plotting each protein in a "tryCatch" call. This would prevent the function from crashing if there's a problem with one protein; you could just print an error in this case and continue plotting the other proteins. (If errors could be avoided, that would be even better, of course.)

The "quantification" function crashes if a protein cannot be quantified due to missing values. In some such cases there may simply be no way to quantify the protein, but this should be handled more gracefully. Again, "tryCatch" could help here, but filtering out the unquantifiable cases beforehand may be even better. For me as a user it is difficult to know which missing data can be tolerated and which which would lead to failures, which is why I believe MSstats should handle this.

I hope you can address these issues.

Cheers

Hendrik

